-- Regression test for smucclaw/l4-ide#915: the EVENT type name used to be
-- shadowed by its own term constructor. The un-renamed `event` builtin surfaces
-- (uppercased) as "EVENT" -- the very name the `eventC` constructor is renamed
-- to. initialEnvironment listed the two under that one key, and built itself
-- with Map.fromList, so the constructor entry (listed second) silently won and
-- the EVENT *type* was dropped. An event-typed signature like
-- `GIVEN e IS AN EVENT ...` was therefore unresolvable on `unstable`.
--
-- initialEnvironment now merges colliding keys (Map.fromListWith) instead of
-- overwriting, so both uniques survive, and the environment's existing
-- multi-candidate resolution narrows them: resolveType keeps only the type
-- candidate, term/constructor resolution keeps only the constructor candidate.
-- This fixture confirms the EVENT type name resolves: the signatures below name
-- it, a CONSIDER destructures an EVENT value, and the #EVALs exercise both.
DECLARE Party IS ONE OF alice, bob
DECLARE Act IS ONE OF ping, pong

GIVEN e IS AN EVENT Party Act
GIVETH A Party
whoActed e MEANS
  CONSIDER e
  WHEN EVENT p a n THEN p

GIVEN e IS AN EVENT Party Act
GIVETH A NUMBER
whenActed e MEANS
  CONSIDER e
  WHEN EVENT p a n THEN n

#EVAL whoActed (EVENT alice ping 5)   -- expect alice
#EVAL whenActed (EVENT bob pong 42)   -- expect 42
